    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d127">

                <pb facs="0337" n="201" sameAs="frus:frus1929v01/pg201"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A15a3/130: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Ambassador in Belgium</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Gibson</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1929-08-26T16:00:00-04:00">August 26, 1929—4
                        p.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>57. In Dawes’ 242 to us Prime Minister says <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“I have been working upon the prospects held out at Geneva. My papers
                            record that on the twenty-third ultimo April Mr. Gibson informed our
                            delegate at Geneva that the plan then suggested would give the American
                            navy superiority over the British of one or two 10,000 eight inch
                            cruisers and give the British navy superiority over America of some
                            thirty 6,400 ton six inch cruisers. That I have met generously.”</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <p>Please inform us if you said it, or what you said. Are we correct in assuming
                    that if you said anything like that you are counting four <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Hawkins</hi> as practically equivalent to three 10,000
                    ton cruisers and that you were limiting your British replacement program to the
                    then announced building program?</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Stimson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
